{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["<b>And he fell on his face.</b> Because of the dispute — for this was already the fourth offense in hand: they sinned with the calf — ״וַיְחַל מֹשֶׁה״ (Exodus 32); with the complainers — ״וַיִּתְפַּלֵּל מֹשֶׁה״ (Numbers 11); with the spies — ״וַיֹּאמֶר מֹשֶׁה אֶל ה' וְשָׁמְעוּ מִצְרַיִם״ (ibid. 14); with the dispute of Korah, his hands went slack. A parable: to a king's son who offended against his father, and his friend appeased on his behalf once and twice and three times — when he offended a fourth time, the hands of that friend went slack. He said: How long shall I trouble the king? Perhaps he will no longer accept from me (Tanchuma)."], :book :numbers, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 16, :notes {}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T09:11:11.734521419Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}